A||||34||DaRkLoRD||SpiralMax?||||1||
Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||01-05-2000||03:43 PM||||Has anyone heard about an aftermarket device called a SpiralMax?  <A HREF="http://www.spiralmax.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.spiralmax.com</A> <P>It is a stainless steel thing that fits inside your air intake tube and swirls the air for better performance.<BR>On the feedback page, there was a comment from a person with a Fiero:<P>"Thank You! SpiralMax. This device made my Pontiac Fiero perform smoothly along with a much faster acceleration. I honestly felt an unbelievable 20 HP gain from this inexpensive device. I have gotten better mileage and I also saved a lot of gas. It paid me back gas within 2 months. It felt like I didn't spend a dime for this device. This is an incredible deal and so do my friends." --B.J. Iiagan-- San Bernardino, CA<P>The device is $90, and looks fairly simple. comments?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steven||01-05-2000||04:10 PM||||Showed that thing to a guy here at work and was told that he saw them tested on Motor Trend Television. MT said that they did not get any improvements in the vehicles tested. He was bummed to hear that, cuase he was ready to buy one himself. I guess that old saying about things that sound to good to be true is still in effect.<BR>Steve<BR>||||reg||13
Z||000002||deceler8||01-05-2000||04:13 PM||||Looks like it would actually be restrictive.<P>If you're gonna spend $80 on that $.25 worth of stainless, I've got an apartment building in Grozny I'll let you have cheap.||||reg||13
Z||000003||mshill||01-05-2000||04:55 PM||||Sounds like the 'Tornado' device advertised in JC Whitney and other auto parts catalogs.  It never made a whole lot of sense to me in a MPFI application, since the theory was to better atomize the fuel, which already happens with MPFI at the intake valve.<P>Now if you could make something like that spin inside your intake... uh, yeah, that would be a supercharger, duh...<BR>||||reg||13
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||01-05-2000||06:25 PM||||I've read a bit about the work done in the early 20th century by a guy named Viktor Shauberger (austrian I believe)<P>his work involved water and air vortices, and showed that a vortex could move water and air faster than a straight pipe of a larger diameter. I can see how their exhaust product might work in light of this, (I wasn't planning to buy one tho. not for $80) <BR>but the comments about putting 2 in line for double the enhancement doesn't make sense. unless it was perfectly aligned with the air streams, it would just disrupt any flow created by the first one.<P>thanks for the feedback.<p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 01-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bruce||01-05-2000||06:52 PM||||Why not just buy the "baffle eliminator" that Tom, from the forum, is selling. It's only $30, and seems to offer essentially what you want.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||01-05-2000||07:15 PM||||A 20 hp increase by putting a restriction in the intake where no air to fuel mixing takes place.....I don't think so.  What a crock.||||reg||1
Z||000007||loafer87gt||01-05-2000||07:20 PM||||One the guys on the Fiero list bought one of these things along with an electric supercharger thingy and say that he gained 36 HP. He said he even dynoed the car before and after the mods. 36 HP is a big gain and if a person can get it for under $500 it would be a hell of a deal. Until I see the dyno sheets though I'm gonna wait.||||reg||1
Z||000008||DRH||01-05-2000||07:51 PM||||OK, the SpiralMax sounds like snake oil to me.<P>Loafer, what is "an electric supercharger thingy"? That sounds a little more convincing. I've wondered from time to time why you couldn't get a small to moderate amount of boost by putting an electric fan upstream of the throttle similar to a turbo. <P>If the 36HP dyno results holds up it would be interesting to know how much EACH mod did. Also, if anything else was changed between the 2 runs.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Scratchy||01-05-2000||08:24 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.electricsupercharger.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.electricsupercharger.com/</A> <P>maybe you want to have a look here...personnally, well, I'm skeptic...<P>||||reg||4
Z||000011||Scratchy||01-05-2000||08:27 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.turbo-zet.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.turbo-zet.com/</A> <P><BR>oops...and here!!!||||reg||4
Z||000012||88formula||01-05-2000||10:20 PM||||Consumer Reports did a test on a similar product called the Tornado on three cars. On two of them it did nothing and on one it cost the vehicle to lose five horses. The Tornado is a spiral shaped device that goes inside your intake tube. I think it's a scam.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Dave85||01-06-2000||12:19 AM||||$300.00 for 5-15 ponies!------NOT!!!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||2FAST4U||01-06-2000||01:10 AM||||Gentlemen:<P>I have been working on a product very similar to this. After a very complete and thorough 23 minute long test and evaluation session I have found that my new invention produces incredible results. I call my new invention The Super Duper Thingamajiggy. For the astonishingly low price of only $99.95 you can be the first one to put a Thingamajiggy on your car. Show it off to all the girls. Kick sand in that bullies face. Yes, in only five minutes too you can install a Thingamajiggy on your car and be the man you allways new you could be.<BR>Just send you money to the Lord but please use my address.<p>[This message has been edited by 2FAST4U (edited 01-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||mshill||01-06-2000||05:16 AM||||Scratchy,<P>I have always wondered about electric superchargers since way back in the late seventies I saw one for a VW beetle in a JC Whitney catalog.  It basically looked like a modern day blow drier.<P>Of the two models (e-Ram and Turbo-Zet) neither of them seemed complete.  The e-Ram only came on at full throttle, so it would in fact be somewhat of an obstruction when you are not racing. I did, however, like the full inline design of the e-Ram where the Turbo-Zet design only provide the fan to push  air through half of the tube, I just don't think this would be effective.  The Turbo-Zet being controlled by a microcontroller seemed to make the Turbo-Zet usable throughout the driving range, instead of just at full throttle.<P>It appear that what is needed is an inline electric blower like the e-Ram that has a variable speed controller to provide boost on acceleration (ie. TPS is opening or at full throttle), zero positive boost, but no vacuum either, at constant RPM (TPS is constant) and shutdown on deceleration ( TPS is closing).<P>It seems that it wouldn't be too hard to program this into a microcontroller and drive a motor from the microcontroller.<P>I may need to bone up on how turbos control the boost under various throttle conditions and what the big gotchas are.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 01-06-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000016||Carrolles||01-06-2000||09:11 AM||||Axial fans can only boost pressure by a couple of inches water column.  This is nowhere near the 7 to 15 PSI that is typically provided by superchargers and turbochargers.<BR>It doesn't matter how fast the fan pushes the air, if it isn't capable of boosting the pressure it won't get the additional mass of air into the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Spyder||01-06-2000||02:15 PM||||The way I see it. The blower is not to create boost. Not forcing air in, It helps move in the extra air when the engine needs it. I doubt it would be worth it on a car unless your racing.  I installed two blowers on my jet ski  for a top end 1mph gain (which is great) and seems like better acceleration. The engine compartment of the ski is designed not to let water in so the air intake is restrictive. The same “boost” is be achieved by  leaving the engine hood cracked open allowing more air to flow in. So what I’m saying is having the air available for the engine to use is what you want.<BR>As far as horsepower, if you add up all the ‘horsepower claimed’ by the manufactures of the gizmos and exhaust systems, performance chips, intake, bla bla bla, my old four cylinder would have 500 horsepower.<BR>You would probably see a better gain by driving around with a quarter tank of gas, or lose that extra 20 lbs. from the holiday eating.||||reg||1
Z||000018||deceler8||01-06-2000||02:19 PM||||I agree...the dumb stuff a lot of people neglect (proper air pressure in tires, clean air filter, proper oil type and viscosity, good, properly gapped plugs, clean injectors and throttle body, proper timing) often do lots more for performance and drivability at little cost than all the expensive add-ons.||||reg||1
Z||000019||DRH||01-06-2000||03:10 PM||||Carrolles, I think you just hit the nail on the head. I found a conversion program and 2" (water) of static pressure is only equal to 0.07225 PSI.<P>I'm thinking about the best one could hope for is the equivalent of having a Ram Air scoop (only it would have the effect from a dead stop).<P>I guess if you could get "free" HP, GM would have done it to begin with.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||mshill||01-06-2000||05:19 PM||||DRH, unfortunately, I never have believed that GM (or any other US car manufacturer) cares about extra HP or even reliability for that matter.  Look at the exhaust on our Fieros... the manifolds could have easily been designed for proper air flow to begin with and gained a cheap 5-7 HP.  They have one thing in mind $$$.  They have a certain budget and every penny is accounted for to meet their profit goals and if it costs them another dollar to clean out the exhaust manifolds they wouldn't spend it.||||reg||1
Z||000021||DRH||01-06-2000||05:50 PM||||mshill, you're right. When I ground out my manifolds I remember thinking what a poorly executed design it is. The engineers probably got stuck on that one. It would have taken a LOT of manual labor to grind out the current design (WAY over a dollar). There probably wasn't enough volume in V6s to justify creating a design that could be automated. Why would management allow extra cost for a few HP when it was being sold to them as a commuter car anyway? Corporate politics.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  BTW, my experience was that 5-7 HP for that mod is very believable.<P>I guess what I meant is that I've NEVER seen an electric SC on ANY factory car. If it was a cost effective way of making power, seems like SOMEONE (maybe not US) would have done it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||2birds||01-06-2000||10:08 PM||||I've got some yellow paint and a few coffee cans to sell real cheap, if you really want a horsepower increase.<P>Just kidding (it only works on imports and neons).<P>My new Car and Driver (Feb 2000)has an article on ethanol powered experimental vehicles, and one of them has an electric powered turbo, from a company called Turbodine Technologies.  Only used at WOT, due to the current draw.  Basically, it's the air side of a turbo and is activated like nitrous, via a microswitch or from the TPS.<P>As far as boost pressure goes, I think the important part is moving more air in AND out of the engine.  Boost is mostly a function of backpressure.  The T-bird Club guys say that when you open up the exhaust, the max boost pressure goes down, but power goes up (saves head gaskets, too).  I would think this theory would also apply to a non-positive displacement blower (axial or centrifugal) also.   ||||reg||1
Z||000023||FieroSTETZ||01-07-2000||02:44 PM||||hey... what if you had a tank of compressed oxygen injecting into the airstream? just wondering, because NOS works because it has more oxygen when combusting... just wondering||||reg||1
Z||000024||2birds||01-07-2000||10:17 PM||||I think nitrous oxide is used because the Nitrogen keeps it from exploding in the intake, like a buffer, but I'm sure there are a lot more knowledgable/experienced people who can enlighten us both.  Any takers?||||reg||1
Z||000025||Phil||01-07-2000||10:53 PM||||Pure O2 and oil will just about ignite on contact. That is why you see "use no oil" on pressure gages that could be exposed to high concentrations of O2||||reg||1
Z||000026||theogre||01-08-2000||02:47 PM||||Phil,<P>The "Use No Oil" warning is there for other reasons. You'll find it on allot of gauges. Biggest reason is that it contaminates the meters moving parts and screws up the calibration. It can even make the gauge freeze.<P>In a properly working gauge the oil would never come in contact with what the gauge is measuring.<P>Pure O2 is very dangerous especially under pressure. Just look at the Apolo 1 disaster.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Phil||01-08-2000||08:12 PM||||I know that the oil should not be able to come in contact with the gas, but getting oil on the threads  or just in the vinicity of high O2 concentrations (leaks etc.) could be an accident waiting to happen.  Sent a few years working in a arsenal and you would be suprised how materials "migrate" and then a few years at Foxboro desiging and making gages and instruments. We found that using my explaination  of the oil O2 mix made a longer lasting impression. "Corporate lies"||||reg||1
Z||000028||fierosound||01-09-2000||03:35 AM||||There is also a manufacturer of an electric turbocharger - TurboDyne.  These unit are said to provide 8PSI of boost. <A HREF="http://www.turbodyne.com" TARGET=_blank>www.turbodyne.com</A> <P>Saw this on a Camaro forum<BR>"I just got the February issue of Car and Driver today. I found an article about 2 GM Silverado pickups modified by two college engineering teams. One pickup used a small dual turbo setup and produced 480hp with 5psi of intercooled boost out of a 5.7L Vortec.<P>The other truck used an electrically driven supercharger made by Turbodyne technologies to produce 360hp with 3.5psi of boost on a similar 5.7L Vortec engine. <P>Stock hp before mods is 270hp."<P>||||reg||10
Z||000029||Phil||01-09-2000||10:21 AM||||2birds<BR>Let's not make fun of yellow paint and coffee can exhaust as I have both on my Formula but I think I can back it up.||||reg||1
Z||000030||2birds||01-09-2000||05:40 PM||||No offense meant, Phil.  Aren't the yellow Formulas and GT's faster than the rest?||||reg||1
Z||000031||olympic||01-09-2000||10:05 PM||||   There are a couple of reasons why nitrous is used instead of pure oxygen. First, nitrous will not ignite until heated to high temps(reducing fire and explosion risks). Second, it cools the combustion chamber reducing detonation.  I've injected pure O2 from my acetylene torch into a car and all that happened was the idle picked up a bit, you'd need lots of oxygen to make more power.<BR>I tried this on my motorcycle, a 125cc Kawasaki, and the engine rpm went so high it scared me.||||reg||1
Z||000032||Phil||01-09-2000||10:32 PM||||Actually neither Nitrous nor O2 are combustable  they promote combustion.||||reg||1
Z||000033||Cooter||01-09-2000||11:25 PM||||Phil,<BR>Thank you for that last post.I have tried to convince my wife of that but she won't believe it. I have even shown her by holding a lit lighter in front of the torch with O2 flowing. Sometimes seeing is still not believing.||||reg||1
Z||000034||DRH||01-10-2000||02:37 AM||||NO2 kits add a fuel injector also. Adding Oxygen without adding fuel would just lean the mixture.||||reg||1
Z||000035||Standard||01-11-2000||03:38 PM||||I knew there was a reason you turned on the red hose first...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
